# Erdős Problem #99 — exclusion of sublinear train-isoperimetric slack

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Repository base:** `b195184e2f2759d0db7c08cb1569f734557540cc`
**State version:** `2026-09-14.audit.51`
**Run type:** closing-proof reduction / extension of the canonical endpoint geometry
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core branch
**Claim class:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit/promotion
**External literature:** no new import; uses the already-audited Fox--Pach--Suk input inside `C-FULLCORE-FLOOR-ENDPOINT-EXCLUSION`
**Computation:** non-load-bearing constant check only

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

This note strengthens the post-endpoint train-slack analysis. It proves that the
train-isoperimetric slack cannot even be sublinear in a canonical full-core Type-II
survivor sequence.

Write

\[
\sigma_D:=B_4-4\sqrt{R_4}\ge0,
\]

where `R_4` is the number of strict-rhombus faces and `B_4=2T` is their
occurrence-counted train boundary. Then there is no canonical full-core sequence with

\[
\sigma_D=o(D).
\]

Consequently there are non-explicit constants `epsilon_sigma>0` and `D_sigma` such
that every sufficiently large canonical full-core survivor satisfies

\[
\boxed{\sigma_D\ge\epsilon_\sigma D.}
\]

Equivalently, the global stopped-train crossing graph has a uniform quadratic Mantel
deficit:

\[
\boxed{
M_D:=\frac{T^2}{4}-R_4\ge c_\sigma D^2
}
\]

for some non-explicit `c_sigma>0` and all sufficiently large full-core survivors.

This does not close C-II by itself, but it removes the near-Mantel train branch
throughout the entire high-surplus regime, not only at the excluded scalar floor
endpoint.

## 1. Candidate chain to the source-level contradiction

The exact implication is

```text
sigma_D=o(D)
  -> T/D -> 2 sqrt(Delta_2)
  -> Mantel deficit o(D^2)
  -> the phase-cut retained train graph is still Mantel-extremal
  -> the canonical endpoint-completion geometry supplies two unequal phase shores
       with total size >=(3/4-o(1))T and each size >=(1/4-o(1))T
  -> almost-full complete actual pseudosegment biclique
  -> remove o(D) reverse/right-angle and phase-tail trains
  -> canonical Section 76:
       D >= (sqrt(3)/2)(3/4 T)-O(1)
  -> D >= (1+eta)D-O(1), contradiction.
```

The final contradiction is with the diameter of the original source. No source points
are deleted, and the cloning used inside the already-audited pseudosegment theorem is
only a graph-theoretic proof device.

## 2. Canonical full-core inputs

Work along a hypothetical canonical full-core Type-II sequence with `D->infinity` and

\[
\sigma_D=o(D).
\tag{SL.1}
\]

The canonical full-core near-disk/rhombic-saturation theorem gives

\[
\frac{R_4}{D^2}\longrightarrow
\Delta_2:=\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3}.
\tag{SL.2}
\]

The train theorem gives

\[
B_4=2T,
\qquad
R_4\le\frac{T^2}{4}.
\tag{SL.3}
\]

The post-endpoint train-slack identity is only algebra applied to (SL.3):

\[
\sigma_D=2T-4\sqrt{R_4}.
\tag{SL.4}
\]

Hence (SL.1)--(SL.2) imply

\[
T=2\sqrt{R_4}+o(D)
=(2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+o(1))D.
\tag{SL.5}
\]

Define the Mantel deficit

\[
M_D:=\frac{T^2}{4}-R_4.
\]

Rationalizing (SL.4) gives the exact finite identity

\[
\sigma_D={8M_D\over T+2\sqrt{R_4}}.
\tag{SL.6}
\]

Since `T=Theta(D)`, (SL.1) and (SL.6) imply

\[
M_D=o(D^2),
\qquad
\frac{R_4}{T^2}\to\frac14.
\tag{SL.7}
\]

Thus (SL.2), (SL.5), and (SL.7) reproduce exactly the endpoint train asymptotics
(1.1) used by the canonical `C-FULLCORE-FLOOR-ENDPOINT-EXCLUSION` proof, without any
assumption on `mu_D`.

## 3. The phase-cut retained graph remains near-Mantel

Canonical `C-PUNCTURED-PHASE` supplies one reduced phase cut deleting only `O(D)`
strict-rhombus crossing pairs. Let `G_D^o` be the retained crossing graph. Then

\[
e(G_D^o)=R_4-O(D)
=\frac{T^2}{4}-o(D^2).
\tag{SL.8}
\]

It is simple and triangle-free. The elementary Mantel-stability lemma used in the
canonical endpoint proof therefore yields a partition

\[
\mathcal T_D=X_D\sqcup Y_D
\]

with

\[
|X_D|=\frac T2+o(T),
\qquad
|Y_D|=\frac T2+o(T),
\tag{SL.9}
\]

and only `o(T^2)` missing retained cross pairs.

The phase cut gives every train one label in `Z/3Z`. Same-label trains cannot be
retained crossing pairs. Therefore the exact three-label allocation lemma from the
canonical endpoint proof applies unchanged. There are distinct labels and shores
`A_D subset X_D`, `B_D subset Y_D` with

\[
|A_D|+|B_D|\ge\frac34T-o(T),
\qquad
\min\{|A_D|,|B_D|\}\ge\frac14T-o(T),
\tag{SL.10}
\]

and

\[
|A_D||B_D|-e_{G_D^o}(A_D,B_D)=o(T^2).
\tag{SL.11}
\]

Both shores are `Theta(D)` by (SL.5).

## 4. Endpoint completion uses no scalar-floor equality

From this point onward, the canonical endpoint proof depends only on
(SL.10)--(SL.11), the canonical phase cut, the train pseudosegment property, the global
`O(D)` angular-defect budget, and Section 76. None depends on `mu_D`.

For completeness, the steps are:

1. The already-audited unbalanced Fox--Pach--Suk cloning lemma turns crossing density
   `1-o(1)` in (SL.11) into complete actual-crossing subfamilies retaining a
   `1-o(1)` fraction of each shore.
2. For the two fixed distinct phase labels, every reverse-oriented or ambiguous
   right-angle crossing lies in a threshold/chain graph with only `O(D)` edges.
   Its vertex cover has size `O(sqrt(D))`, so deleting it loses `o(D)` trains.
3. On the resulting complete forward grid the total angular defect is `O(D)`.
   The endpoint phase-tail argument deletes only `O(D^(1/4))=o(D)` additional trains
   and puts every remaining reduced phase into one interval of width `<pi/6`, exactly
   the common-cone hypothesis needed by canonical Section 76.

Thus there are complete forward phase-pure shores of sizes `a_D,b_D` satisfying

\[
a_D+b_D\ge\frac34T-o(T),
\qquad
\min(a_D,b_D)\ge\frac14T-o(T),
\tag{SL.12}
\]

with crossing defect sum `S_D=O(D)`.

## 5. Source-hull diameter contradiction

Canonical Section 76 gives

\[
D\ge {\sqrt3\over2}(a_D+b_D)
-{S_D\over\min(a_D,b_D)}-2.
\tag{SL.13}
\]

The defect quotient is `O(1)`. By (SL.5) and (SL.12),

\[
\frac{a_D+b_D}{D}
\ge {3\over4}{T\over D}-o(1)
=\frac32\sqrt{\Delta_2}-o(1).
\tag{SL.14}
\]

Therefore

\[
D\ge
\left[
{3\sqrt3\over4}\sqrt{\Delta_2}-o(1)
\right]D-O(1).
\tag{SL.15}
\]

But

\[
{3\sqrt3\over4}\sqrt{\Delta_2}>1
\iff
\Delta_2>{16\over27},
\]

and

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}>{3\over4}>{16\over27}.
\tag{SL.16}
\]

Hence (SL.15) says `D>=(1+eta)D-O(1)` for some fixed `eta>0`, impossible for
large `D`. This contradicts (SL.1).

### Theorem SL.1

There is no canonical full-core Type-II sequence with `D->infinity` and

\[
\boxed{\sigma_D=o(D).}
\tag{SL.17}
\]

## 6. Uniform linear gap and quadratic Mantel deficit

The sequence exclusion upgrades to a uniform asymptotic gap. If no
`epsilon_sigma>0` existed, then for every integer `j` one could choose a canonical
full-core survivor with

\[
D_j\ge j,
\qquad
0\le {\sigma_{D_j}\over D_j}< {1\over j},
\]

producing a forbidden sequence with `sigma_D=o(D)`. Therefore there are
non-explicit constants `epsilon_sigma>0,D_sigma` such that

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_D\ge\epsilon_\sigma D
\quad(D\ge D_\sigma)
}
\tag{SL.18}
\]

throughout the canonical full-core branch.

Using (SL.6) in the equivalent form

\[
M_D={\sigma_D(T+2\sqrt{R_4})\over8}
\ge {\sigma_D\sqrt{R_4}\over4},
\]

and (SL.2), one gets another non-explicit constant `c_sigma>0` with

\[
\boxed{
M_D\ge c_\sigma D^2
}
\tag{SL.19}
\]

for all sufficiently large full-core survivors.

Thus every genuine high-surplus full-core survivor is uniformly separated from the
Mantel endpoint at the quadratic crossing scale.

## 7. Interaction with the post-endpoint five-currency split

The exact post-endpoint identity was

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mu_DD
={}&{2p+2\sqrt{R_4}\over5}
+m_B+{2\over5}(Q-f)+{2\over5}(b-p)\\
&+{1\over10}\rho_D+{1\over10}\sigma_D
-2c_I-4.
\end{aligned}
\tag{SL.20}
\]

Before this note, `sigma_D` was one of five possible places for the positive
post-endpoint gap to live, and the `sigma_D=o(D)` case led to a highly structured
near-Mantel carrier. Theorem SL.1 rules that case out completely. Instead, every
sufficiently large full-core survivor itself has

\[
\sigma_D=\Omega(D),
\qquad
M_D=\Omega(D^2).
\]

The next live question is therefore no longer how to complete a near-Mantel train
system. It is how a uniformly positive quadratic Mantel deficit is paid by phase,
interface, detour, or source-level diameter currency.

## 8. Scope audit

This note does **not**:

- alter the macroscopic-container branch;
- give an explicit numerical value for `epsilon_sigma`;
- upper-bound the aggregate outer-walk detour;
- construct a strict sub-`D` same-cardinality source;
- force an exact unit triangle.

It retains all contact-isolated source points. The final Section-76 inequality is a
source-hull inequality on actual strict-rhombus centers, exactly as in the canonical
endpoint audit. The only new bridge is the observation that `sigma_D=o(D)` itself
reproduces all endpoint train asymptotics needed by that audited geometric proof.
